WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.620
When the skin of a mouse's hindpaw is indented, what climbs the spinal cord?

2
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:09.680
The textbook draws a cable.

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.620
Touch spikes enter the dorsal column and run up to the brainstem.

4
00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:16.300
Cats showed this branch decades ago.

5
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.520
Some of the spikes have already been through the cord's own circuitry.

6
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:22.473
One route really is direct.

7
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:25.733
Touch afferents are the sensory fibres from the skin.

8
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:30.513
Some run up the dorsal column with no synapse on the way, and reach the gracile

9
00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:31.833
nucleus in the brainstem.

10
00:00:32.573 --> 00:00:37.753
In anaesthetized adult mice, this direct input carried vibration with high temporal

11
00:00:37.753 --> 00:00:38.753
precision.

12
00:00:39.317 --> 00:00:43.477
Touch afferents also end in the dorsal horn, a layered region of the cord.

13
00:00:44.137 --> 00:00:46.857
The layers are called laminae, numbered from the surface.

14
00:00:47.637 --> 00:00:51.497
In the mouse lumbar cord, about 70 percent of the neurons in this zone are

15
00:00:51.497 --> 00:00:54.617
excitatory, and about 30 percent are inhibitory.

16
00:00:55.277 --> 00:01:01.237
A postsynaptic dorsal column neuron, or PSDC neuron, gets its excitation mainly from

17
00:01:01.237 --> 00:01:02.237
these local neurons.

18
00:01:02.237 --> 00:01:04.877
It sends an axon up the dorsal column.

19
00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:10.570
Two kinds of inhibition shape what the PSDC neuron sends.

20
00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:15.930
In feedforward inhibition, the same afferent input that excites the neuron also

21
00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.050
excites an interneuron that inhibits it.

22
00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:23.490
In presynaptic inhibition, the inhibition lands on the afferent terminal and turns

23
00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.030
down the transmitter it releases.

24
00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:30.890
In anaesthetized adult mice, a genetic knockout of either one enlarged the receptive

25
00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.130
fields of these neurons.

26
00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:36.373
Now compare arrival times, using illustrative numbers.

27
00:01:37.053 --> 00:01:41.133
The direct route is 0.08 m at 16 m/s.

28
00:01:41.553 --> 00:01:46.813
That takes 5 ms. The indirect route needs 2.5 ms to reach the cord.

29
00:01:47.413 --> 00:01:50.853
Two synapses, at an assumed 1 ms each, add 2.

30
00:01:51.413 --> 00:01:55.773
The PSDC axon, at an assumed 8 m/s, adds 5.

31
00:01:55.773 --> 00:02:02.393
The total is 9.5 ms, so the processed copy arrives 4.5 ms later.

32
00:02:03.433 --> 00:02:04.973
The two copies meet again.

33
00:02:05.493 --> 00:02:09.673
In anaesthetized adult mice, the direct and indirect inputs to single

34
00:02:09.673 --> 00:02:14.033
thalamus-projecting neurons in the gracile nucleus had highly aligned receptive

35
00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:17.093
fields. The direct input carried vibration.

36
00:02:17.873 --> 00:02:21.873
The indirect input carried sustained indentation, up to high forces.

37
00:02:22.653 --> 00:02:24.513
Both contributed to gentle displacement.

